<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
    <h3>Add the attribute to the test</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-15|6|9-12">
use Symfony\Bridge\PhpUnit\Attribute\UseRecord;
use Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Test\WebTestCase;

class GetRandomControllerTest extends WebTestCase
{
    #[UseRecord]
    public function testRandom(): void
    {
        $client = static::createClient();
        $client->request('GET', '/random');

        $this->assertResponseIsSuccessful();
    }
}
    </code></pre>
    <p class="fragment subtitle" style="font-size: 0.6em;">The test uses <code>GetRandomControllerTest/testRandom.har</code>, stored next to it. Nothing else changes in the code.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Step two. Here's a completely ordinary functional test: a WebTestCase, we create a client, we call /random, a route whose controller calls an external API, and we check that the response is successful.</p>
        <p>[click] The only thing we add is the #[UseRecord] attribute on the test method.</p>
        <p>[click] And the rest of the test doesn't change. No mock to inject, no service to replace in the container.</p>
        <p>[click] The test uses GetRandomControllerTest/testRandom.har, in a directory named after the class, next to the test.</p>
        <p>You may have noticed that we configured nothing to enable the recorder. That's normal: it's enabled by default when framework.test is true, so in the test environment, and it doesn't even exist in production. If you don't want it, one line is enough: recorder: false under http_client. And enabling it everywhere is safe: without #[UseRecord] on a test, the recorder does nothing, it lets everything through.</p>
        <p>The important point: the attribute only names the file. It doesn't decide whether we record. Recording is decided when you launch the tests, with the environment variable. That way, nothing that touches the network can be committed by mistake.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
